==============================================================================
S8. 1-cycles (1^(L-1), k) and 2-cycles (1^a k1 1^b k2): where the gate opens, both sheets
==============================================================================
1-cycle words (1^(L-1), k), L <= 3000, K in floor(L log_2 3)+{-1,0,1,2}, positive integral, primitive:
  b=+1: [(1, 2, [1])]
  b=-1: [(1, 1, [1]), (2, 2, [5, 7])]
Plus sheet: only the trivial word (2) -> {1}; Steiner 1977 (CITED) proves this for ALL L.
Minus sheet: {5,7} IS a nontrivial 1-cycle (word (1,2)), so Steiner's theorem is SIGN-SPECIFIC
and its transfer to 3n-1 is REFUTED by the witness {5,7}; its Baker/continued-fraction
machinery is sheet-blind, but its conclusion is not.
2-cycle words 1^a k1 1^b k2 (k1,k2>=2), L <= 24: 9699 words tested
  b=+1: positive cycles reached: [[1]]
  b=-1: positive cycles reached: [[5, 7], [17, 25, 37, 41, 55, 61, 91]]
Minus sheet: the seven-cycle (1,1,1,2,1,1,4) is a genuine 2-cycle; Simons-de Weger's
'no m-cycles, m<=68' is therefore SIGN-SPECIFIC (transfer REFUTED by the seven-cycle, m=2).
The repeated (1,2)(1,2) also appears as a non-primitive 2-cycle word for {5,7}.

