S9. Unit-gap clocks |2^K-3^L| = 1 (Catalan/Mihailescu) carry EVERY word; necklace counts
==============================================================================
clocks with |Delta| = 1, L <= 400: [(1, 1, -1), (2, 1, 1), (3, 2, -1)]
clocks with |Delta| <= 100, L <= 400: 9 of them: [(1, 1, -1), (2, 1, 1), (3, 2, -1), (4, 2, 7), (4, 3, -11), (5, 3, 5), (6, 4, -17), (7, 4, 47), (8, 5, 13)]
Mihailescu 2004 (CITED; Catalan's conjecture): 8 and 9 are the only consecutive perfect powers,
so 3/2 is the last unit-gap clock for all L.  On a unit-gap clock q=1 for every word, so every
composition is a cycle word: (1)->{1} at b=-1 / {-1} at b=+1; (2)->{1} at b=+1; (1,2),(2,1)
-> {5,7} at b=-1 / {-5,-7} at b=+1.  SHEET-BLIND (both sides of every unit gap are used).
  clock 1/1: compositions C(K-1,L-1) = 1, necklaces (rotation classes) = 1
  clock 2/1: compositions C(K-1,L-1) = 1, necklaces (rotation classes) = 1
  clock 3/2: compositions C(K-1,L-1) = 2, necklaces (rotation classes) = 1
